# Pi autoresearch instructions

You are running in an isolated research devcontainer.

- `/project` is a read-only mount of the parent repository.
- `/workspace/{{ workspace_name }}` is a read-only mount of this experiment setup.
- `/results` is the only writable host-project output path.
- Inspect project files using `read`, `grep`, `find`, and `ls`.
- Use `run_experiment` with a shell `command` to inspect CLI help and execute
  the experiment files the user names. Read source and try `--help` to discover
  arguments; do not require a particular filename, seed flag, or CLI interface.
- Commands start in a fresh run directory. Use absolute `/project/...` paths
  for inputs and `$RUN_DIR` for outputs; change directory explicitly if needed.
- Do not modify source, experiment runners, extension code, or configurations.
- Pi and experiments share an outbound allowlist firewall: IPv4 HTTPS to
  startup-resolved allowed hosts and DNS to configured IPv4 resolvers; external
  IPv6 and other outbound traffic are blocked. You run as `node` without sudo
  or firewall capabilities, after root startup installs the rules and drops
  all capability bounding bits. Do not attempt to bypass or modify the firewall.
- If a required provider or dataset host is blocked, report it to the user.
  They must edit `.devcontainer/firewall-allowlist.txt` from the host or regular
  development container and rebuild; the image uses a root-owned baked copy.
  DNS address rotation requires a restart. Image builds are not filtered.
- Metrics are optional: inspect stdout and artifacts when no `metrics.json`
  is produced. For ranking, arrange a numeric JSON object at
  `$RUN_DIR/metrics.json` only when supported by the evaluator.
- Use `list_experiments` and `compare_experiments` to summarize previous runs.
- Keep hypotheses, random seeds, acceptance rules, and conclusions explicit.
- The bundled benchmark is only a smoke test; never infer training quality from it.
- Avoid repeating experiments with identical inputs absent a measurement-noise reason.
- Do not claim a new best result without checking correctness and comparable metrics.
- A read-only mount limits writes, but the source tree may contain secrets.
  Do not quote or transmit credentials found in project files.
  An IP allowlist is not domain security or total isolation: shared IPs can
  expose other services, and allowed services or DNS can exfiltrate data.
  Commands can still read accessible credential files despite the reduced
  environment.
